# Pitch, both ways, from a standing craft on the start line.
#
# The scenario that was missing: every capture before this one held the pitch
# axis at zero, so `avel_x` - the pitch entry of the recorded angular column -
# was excited only incidentally, by the track pushing the ship around. See
# `docs/physics/angular-velocity-column.md`.
#
# **Nothing is held but the pitch axis.** No thrust, so the craft never leaves
# the start line, never reaches a wall, and `speed/|velocity|` reads 1.0000 on
# every tick - the cheap cleanliness test from
# `docs/physics/force-balance-ground-truth.md`. That is worth more here than
# realism: a pitch measurement taken while the contact response is eating
# velocity is a measurement of two things.
#
# **`up` on the d-pad pitches the nose DOWN**, measured rather than assumed: it
# writes `-100` to the pitch axis at `*(craft+0x78)+0x10` and the body's forward
# row drops. `down` writes `+100` and the nose rises. Both are the same +/-100
# in-memory scale the steering axis uses, and the analog stick's y feeds the
# same field with the same sign (`stick_y=+1` reads `-100`, like `up`), which
# also settles `oag-trace`'s open question about PPSSPP's analog polarity.
#
# The response is a damped second-order one rather than a sustained rate - the
# hover spring and the surface-alignment torque pull the attitude back - so each
# hold is long enough to see the transient, the overshoot and the settle, and
# each release is long enough for the craft to return to its resting attitude
# before the other direction starts. Turning both ways is the `steer-both-ways`
# lesson applied to this axis: a sign error shows up as the two halves swapping
# rather than as a plausible-looking curve.
40 -
120 up
50 -
120 down
30 -
